## Proof

**Setup.** Let the circle $k$ have center $O$ and radius $1$. Place $O$ at the origin and let $A=(1,0)$. Write $B=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$ on $k$, so the side length of the equilateral triangle is
$$s = AB = 2\sin\tfrac{\theta}{2}.$$
Set $t=\theta/2$, so $s=2\sin t$.

**Locating $D$.** The point $D\neq B$ on $k$ with $AD=s$ is the reflection of $B$ across the diameter $OA$:
$$D=(\cos 2t,\,-\sin 2t).$$

**Locating $C$.** The midpoint of $AB$ is $M=\bigl(\cos^{2}t,\;\sin t\cos t\bigr)$, at distance $\cos t$ from $O$ along the perpendicular bisector of $AB$. The two equilateral vertices lie on this bisector at distance $\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\sqrt3\sin t$ from $M$. The vertex on the side of $AB$ toward $O$ is
$$C = M\bigl(1-\sqrt3\tan t\bigr)=\bigl(\cos t-\sqrt3\sin t\bigr)(\cos t,\sin t).$$
Writing $r=\cos t-\sqrt3\sin t$, we have $C=r(\cos t,\sin t)$ and $|C|=|r|$. One checks $|C|<1$ exactly for $0<t<\pi/3$ (i.e. $C$ is interior), which we assume.

**Using the power of $C$.** Since $C$ lies inside $k$ and the line $DC$ meets $k$ at $D$ and $E$,
$$CD\cdot CE = 1-|C|^{2}.$$

**Computing $|C|^{2}$.**
$$|C|^{2}=(\cos t-\sqrt3\sin t)^{2},\qquad 1-|C|^{2}=1-(\cos t-\sqrt3\sin t)^{2}=2\sin t\,(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t).$$

**Computing $CD$.** With $D=(\cos 2t,-\sin 2t)$ and $C=\bigl(\cos^{2}t-\sqrt3\sin t\cos t,\;\sin t\cos t-\sqrt3\sin^{2}t\bigr)$:
$$\overrightarrow{CD}=\sin t\bigl(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t,\;\sqrt3\sin t-3\cos t\bigr).$$
Hence
$$CD^{2}=\sin^{2}t\Big[(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t)^{2}+(\sqrt3\sin t-3\cos t)^{2}\Big].$$
Expanding the bracket:
$$(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t)^{2}+(\sqrt3\sin t-3\cos t)^{2}=4(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t)^{2},$$
so
$$CD=2\sin t\,(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t) \qquad(\text{positive for }0<t<\pi/3).$$

**Conclusion.**
$$CE=\frac{1-|C|^{2}}{CD}=\frac{2\sin t\,(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t)}{2\sin t\,(\sqrt3\cos t-\sin t)}=1.$$

The length of the line segment $CE$ is $\boxed{1}$.

